s 28…Qh7, which moves the attacked queen to safety.","Better was 28.Nd2 (−1.14): moving away uncovers the bishop on e2, which now hits the queen on h5."],"better":"28.Nd2 wins the queen for a knight, picking it up with 29.Bxh5. After 28…cxd2 29.Bxh5 Rxc2 30.Qxc2 Rc8 31.Qd1 gxh5 32.f3, the rooks are traded and White comes out a piece up (−1.14)."},{"ply":87,"name":"44.Bxc3","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"44.Bxc3 misses 44.Rxg6+, which wins material. White's winning chances go from 50% to 27%.","points":["Black answers 44…bxc3, which takes the bishop on c3.","After bxc3, Black wins 3 points of material within 2 moves.","Better was 44.Rxg6+ (0.00), which takes the queen on g6."],"better":"44.Rxg6+ wins the queen for a rook. After 44…Rxg6 45.Qh5 Rf8 46.Qxd5 Rf5 47.Qc6, White comes out the exchange plus a pawn up (0.00)."},{"ply":90,"name":"45…Nf5","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"45…Nf5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Protects the h4 pawn, which was hanging.","The next best move, 45…Nf7, would drop Black's winning chances from 76% to 63% (−1.47).","45…Nf5 protects the h4 pawn.","White answers 46.Rxg6+, winning the queen for a rook.","After 46.Rxg6+ Rxg6 47.Qh5 Rf8 48.Rc7+ Be7 49.Rxe7+ Nxe7 50.Qxh4 Kf7, Black is a piece down (−3.22)."]}],"opening":{"name":"English Opening: Agincourt Defense","eco":"A13","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 7…Nbd7: 14 moves in all, 7 by White and 7 by Black.","White left the book with 8.Qc2. In 66 games from this position, strong players played 8.Rc1 (39%), 8.d4 (24%), 8.Be2 (12%) or 8.g3 (12%).","The main line goes on 8.Rc1 a6 9.Ne2 Bd6.","From here White scored 64% in those games, with 21% drawn."]}}